WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.980
SPEAKER: This is a
story about networking.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:02.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:03.960
Once upon a time,
when you wanted

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.330
to set up a private
network or a subnet

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.130
to host your workloads
in the Cloud,

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.550
you had to construct a
Virtual Private Cloud, or VPC,

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.990
contained to just one region.

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.250
This was OK, as long as you
were only managing two or three.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.840
But scaling up
became really hard

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.300
because you needed VPNs
between each region

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.410
to keep your traffic secure, and
this meant longer wait times.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.890
At Google, we imagined
a single global VPC,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.680
where VMs and regional subnets
could communicate privately

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:32.790
across the world.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.850
And all of this without
going through a VPN.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.200
And then we built it.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.890
This global VPC approach
allowed for simplicity at scale,

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.830
empowering businesses to
add subnet after subnet

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:47.370
without a resulting network
that was too unwieldy to manage.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.800
For network professionals
who lived in both worlds,

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.680
the difference is,
well, noticeable.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.230
Just ask anybody who spent
time stitching together

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.360
VPN gateway after gateway.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:59.220
Ask anybody who's
had to pinpoint

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.030
the source of a network problem
in a system of localized VPCs

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:04.410
when something breaks.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:06.690
Or anybody who's had to
manage security policies

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.430
for those different VPCs.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.950
Or anyone trying to
maintain clear segregation

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.710
of their engineers
access to each network.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.600
When you use Google's
network, you'll

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.120
reap all the benefits of scaling
without any of these drawbacks.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.390
So, what makes
this all possible?

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.980
Originally, we built
our own network

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.490
because, quite simply, we
couldn't buy for any price

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.200
the scale and
performance we needed.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.780
So what did we have
to do differently

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.610
to solve for this
scale and performance

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.650
when designing our own network?

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.210
A big part of the answer?

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.800
Tail latency.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:40.460
When you're doing any kind
of distributed computation,

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:42.210
you don't just care
about how fast you can

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.610
get the data most of the time.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.310
You care about what happens
to the speed at the tail end

00:01:47.310 --> 00:01:48.990
of the distribution.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.930
To understand why, suppose
you have a computation that

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.870
starts at one server,
collects data, and doesn't

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.080
finish until it collects
data from 1,000 more servers.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.870
If even one of those
servers returns your query

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.000
significantly later
than the rest,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.770
well, the speed of
your computation

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.560
is only as fast as
the slowest server.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.229
But at Google, by guaranteeing
that the worst case is

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:13.320
very close to the best
case, we design our network

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:14.760
for what matters most--

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.590
predictability.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.080
It's this network and
the philosophy driving it

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.110
that allows us to treat your
hundreds, sometimes thousands,

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.900
of servers as one
unit of computation.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.000
One collective brain
talking to itself

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.810
across locations
around the globe.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.690
And what does this mean for you?

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.880
When designing services
for your customers,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.220
you get to assume low latency.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:38.460
In large part, this is thanks
to best-in-class global load

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.680
balancing services,
which support

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:43.590
one-million-plus
queries per second.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.900
Traffic enters Cloud
Global Load Balancing

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:49.000
through 80-plus distinct global
load balancing locations,

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.730
meaning your traffic
primarily stays

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:53.460
on Google's fast private
network backbone instead

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.440
of public internet
service providers.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.810
And naturally, this
design can accommodate

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.460
huge unexpected
spikes in traffic.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:03.310
So when your app becomes a
crowd favorite overnight,

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.430
our network intelligently
directs traffic

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.500
to VM instances that
can be configured

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.170
to spin up automatically
in a matter of seconds.

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.100
In other words, we're not just
hoping your business will grow,

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:15.900
we're counting on it.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.722
Of course, we also know that
one size doesn't fit all,

00:03:18.722 --> 00:03:20.430
and that there will
always be a few niche

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:22.290
cases where you might
still want to set up

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.510
a separate regional VPC.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.460
So we built that
option right in.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:28.350
With Google's network,
you can take advantage

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.600
of our global VPC
when it makes sense,

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.240
and go the traditional
route when you need to.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.740
If you're ready to
see for yourself,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.960
head to cloud.google.com.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.650
But the story doesn't end there.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.890
There's so much more we
could say about networks.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.900
Actually, we created
a whole series,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.660
Cloud Networking End-To-End,
dedicated to answering all

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.030
the questions you've ever
had about networking,

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.590
and some you didn't.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.510
Ever wondered how to
set up your own VPC

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.230
on Google Cloud, hybrid
connectivity, firewall rules,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:57.490
or external load balancers?

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:00.300
So be sure to check out Cloud
Networking End-To-End, as well

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.860
as a bunch of
other great content

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.290
on the Google Cloud
Platform YouTube channel.

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:07.940
[MUSIC PLAYING]

